  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Spring Framework / Spring Security — NO PATCHED RELEASE EXISTS
       Added 2026-09-23 (Session 125 cont. 8). Review by 2026-12-22.

       Every one of these was checked individually against the GitHub Advisory DB
       and NVD: spring-core 6.2.19 and spring-security-core 6.5.11 are the NEWEST
       published versions in their lines, and none of these CVEs has a fixed
       version anywhere (6.2.x, 6.5.x or 7.x). There is no upgrade to take.

       Everything that COULD be fixed by upgrading was fixed by upgrading instead
       of suppressed — see the "Security pins" block in backend/pom.xml and
       card-service/pom.xml (Tomcat 10.1.60, Netty 4.1.138, PostgreSQL 42.7.13,
       httpcore5 5.4.3, httpclient5 5.6.4, log4j2 2.26.1).

       `until` is real: when it passes, dependency-check ignores the suppression
       and the build fails again on that CVE. That is the intended prompt to
       re-check for a patched Spring release. Do NOT bump the date without
       re-running the check — if a fix has shipped, upgrade and DELETE the entry.

       Stack facts these justifications rest on (verified 2026-09-23):
         * Servlet/MVC on Tomcat. 16 @RestController in card-service, all MVC.
         * spring-webflux is on card-service's classpath for WebClient ONLY —
           no @EnableWebFlux, no RouterFunctions, no reactive controllers.
         * Not present anywhere: RSocket, Jetty, Aalto XML, XsltView, WebAuthn,
           UnboundID, SseEmitter/text-event-stream.
         * No @ModelAttribute / WebDataBinder / @InitBinder; all 188 request
           payloads bind as JSON through Jackson.
         * No SpEL evaluation of user input (no SpelExpressionParser /
           parseExpression in either service).
       PURL SCOPE: dependency-check attributes a Spring Framework CVE to EVERY
       sibling spring-* jar via CPE, so suppressing only spring-core simply moved
       the failure to spring-tx. These entries therefore use the group-level PURL
       ^pkg:maven/org\.springframework/.*$ — which is still NARROW in the sense
       the policy cares about: each entry names its exact CVEs, so a different
       future CVE in any spring-* jar is NOT hidden.
       ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

